Atlas Browser (parallel isis-browser fork) — ported fixes
  Copyright (c) Herman van Hazendonk (Herrie82) and the Atlas Browser project.
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
  Origin: https://github.com/Herrie82/BrowserAdapter
          https://github.com/Herrie82/BrowserServer
  Jihad Browser incorporates device-hardening fixes first developed in Atlas
  (which also forks isis-browser but targets WPE WebKit):
    - YAP memory-safety fixes (YapProxy delete[], YapServer int16 length
      validation, YapClient reconnect socket teardown, YapPacket malloc
      null-check) — Atlas BrowserServer commit 89b71a7.
    - BrowserAdapter LunaCE paint fix (dstBuffer raw-blit path instead of the
      QPainter/PGContext deref that segfaults LunaSysMgr), out-of-buffer white
      fill, tall-buffer pan, pinch-zoom scaled blit, and scroll indicator —
      Atlas BrowserAdapter commits 7b91ab8, aefab6d, dbc897c, 27b54a5, 77ab724.
    - BrowserAdapter key-focus gating: handleKeyDown/handleKeyUp returned
      bEditorFocused alone, so a page that auto-focuses an input on load left the
      adapter consuming every keystroke even after the user tapped the chrome
      address bar. Gated on mPageFocused as well — Atlas BrowserAdapter commit
      ce199bc. Jihad extends the same expression with mFlashGestureLock so a Flash
      game (which focuses no HTML editor) also receives its keys instead of
      leaking them to the chrome; that extension is the Jihad project's own.
    - BrowserAdapter rotation-aware composite: useGraphicsContext=true + bitblt
      the offscreen (wrapped as a Piranha PGSurface) THROUGH the WebKit-provided
      PGContext, which carries the card's rotation/scale transform. This makes
      portrait<->landscape rotation render correctly (the raw dstBuffer blit is
      row-major in the card's logical space and sheared/tiled after a rotate) —
      Atlas BrowserAdapter handlePaint graphics-context path. The Piranha PG* ABI
      declarations (render/adapter/PGContext.h, PGSurface.h) are the Jihad
      project's own minimal reconstruction of the webOS SDK interface (resolved at
      load time from the device libWebKitLuna.so), not copied from HP or Atlas.
    - Self-contained install model (packaging/, build/webos-oe/recipes-jihad/):
      run the engine + render daemon IN PLACE from the app's own cryptofs
      deviceroot and copy NOTHING to /media/internal, because that vfat volume is
      the user's USB mass storage and must stay free of app internals; confine the
      rootfs footprint to the NPAPI adapter plugin and the app's own upstart job,
      opened in a single remount-rw window and reversed exactly by prerm. Adopted
      from atlas-browser-app/packaging/{ipk-postinst.sh,ipk-prerm.sh}, including
      the practice of removing only the package's own files on uninstall so stock
      registrations survive. Jihad's variant-namespacing (three independent
      packages) is its own.
    - Low-memory design patterns from atlas-wpe-backend/BrowserPageWPE.cpp:
      pin the engine memory budget near the REAL free memory so purges happen
      before system OOM, throttle pressure handling to avoid GC thrash, and
      persist the cookie jar on disk so consent/login cookies survive daemon
      restarts (EngineHost memory-pressure watcher + profile dir provider).

